'''''Over Eternal Peace''''' is a [[landscape painting]] by Russian artist [[Isaac Levitan]] (1860–1900), completed in 1894. It belongs to the [[State Tretyakov Gallery]] (Inventory No. 1486). The size of the painting is 150 × 206&nbsp;cm<ref>{{Cite book |title=Государственная Третьяковская галерея — каталог собрания |date=2001 |publisher=Красная площадь |isbn=5-900743-56-X |volume=4: Живопись второй половины XIX века, book 1, А—М |location=Moscow |pages=360–361 }}</ref> (152 × 207.5&nbsp;cm, according to other sources).<ref name=":0">{{Cite web |title=Левитан Исаак Ильич — Над вечным покоем. 1894 |url=https://www.tretyakovgallery.ru/collection/nad-vechnym-pokoem/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190502213110/https://www.tretyakovgallery.ru/collection/nad-vechnym-pokoem/ |archive-date=2 May 2019 |access-date=2 October 2019 |publisher=Государственная Третьяковская галерея — www.tretyakovgallery.ru}}</ref>

The painting's creation began in the summer of 1893, while Levitan was in [[Tver Governorate|Tver governorate]], specifically in the area of lakes Ostrovno and [[Udomlya Lake|Udomlya]].{{Sfn|Чижмак|2010|p=67}}<ref name=":3">{{Cite book |title=Исаак Ильич Левитан. Документы, материалы, библиография |date=1966 |publisher=Искусство |location=Moscow |pages=46 }}</ref> In 1894, the canvas ''Over Eternal Peace'' was exhibited at the 22nd exhibition of the [[Peredvizhniki|Society for Travelling Art Exhibitions]] ('Peredvizhniki') in [[Saint Petersburg]].<ref>{{Harvard citation no brackets|Catalogue of the State Tretyakov Gallery, vol. 4, book 1|2001|Ref=Каталог ГТГ, т. 4, кн. 1}}</ref> Prior to the exhibition's opening, [[Pavel Tretyakov]] had already purchased the painting from the author.<ref name=":4">{{Cite book |title=Исаак Ильич Левитан. Документы, материалы, библиография |date=1966 |publisher=Искусство |location=Moscow |pages=48 }}</ref>

''Over Eternal Peace'' is one of the three largest pieces created by the artist, alongside ''[[By the Pool]]'' (1892) and ''[[Lake (painting)|Lake]]'' (1899–1900).{{Sfn|Фёдоров-Давыдов|1966|p=296}}{{Sfn|Фёдоров-Давыдов|1975|p=575—576}} ''Over Eternal Peace'', along with ''By The Pool'' and [[Vladimirka (painting)|''Vladimirka'']] (1892), from the first half of the 1890s, are occasionally grouped together as the 'gloomy' or "dramatic" trilogy by Levitan.{{Sfn|Петров|1992|p=84}}{{Sfn|Петров|2000|p=33—35}}<ref>{{Cite web |title=Исаак Ильич Левитан — Лучшие картины, пейзажи — У омута, 1892 |url=http://isaak-levitan.ru/master/21.php |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120409201834/http://isaak-levitan.ru/master/21.php |archive-date=9 April 2012 |access-date=31 August 2019 |publisher=isaak-levitan.ru}}</ref>

According to the writer and publicist [[Vasily Mikheev]], the canvas ''Over Eternal Peace'' is "a true landscape painting," this work by Levitan – "a symphony, strange at first sight, but subtly embracing the soul once you trust its impression."{{Sfn|Михеев|1894|p=125—126}} Art historian [[Aleksei Fedorov-Davydov]] wrote that ''Over Eternal Peace'' explores "the relationship between human existence and the eternal life of nature," and "from this juxtaposition of nature and the traces of human existence in it, a landscape filled with sublime sorrow and tragic heroics is formed."{{Sfn|Фёдоров-Давыдов|1966|p=172}} According to art historian [[Vitaly Serafimovich Manin|Vitaly Manin]], the painting is "one of the artist's most expressive works, dynamic and 'associative'."{{Sfn|Манин|2001|p=229}}

== History ==

=== Creation of the painting ===
Isaac Levitan began working on the painting ''Over Eternal Peace'' in the summer of 1893 while living in [[Tver Governorate|Tver governorate]].{{Sfn|Чижмак|2010|p=67}}<ref name=":3" /> First, he was invited to visit the Garino estate on the [[Msta|Msta River]] by artist [[Lavrentiy Dmitrievich Donskoy|Lavrentiy Donskoy]].{{Sfn|Подушков|2009}} Then, Levitan arrived at Troitsa station{{Sfn|Пророкова|1960|p=142}}{{Sfn|Подушков|2009}} (now [[Udomlya]]), that was a part of [[Moscow-Vindavo-Rybinsk Railway]] at the time,<ref>{{Cite web |last=Подушков |first=Д.Л. |title=Удомля. Историческая справка |url=http://starina.tverlib.ru/us-000.htm |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180801211533/http://starina.tverlib.ru/us-000.htm |archive-date=1 August 2018 |access-date=2 October 2019 |publisher=Удомельская старина — starina.tverlib.ru}}</ref> by train with his companion, the artist [[Sofia Kuvshinnikova]]. Upon arrival, Filipp Petrov, a peasant from the village of Doronino, recommended that they stay at the Ushakovs' estate Ostrovno, as they welcomed holidaymakers. Levitan followed Petrov's advice and stayed at Ostrovno. The estate was situated on the shore of Lake Ostrovno, which derives its name from the three islands located within it.{{Sfn|Подушков|2014|pp=116—126}}
[[File:Udomlya Ostrovno.jpg|left|thumb|Lakes Udomlya and Ostrovno on the [[topographic map]] of 1853]]
The estate was owned by Ekaterina Nikolayevna Ushakova (née Seslavina, 1821–1910) and her children Varvara Vladimirovna (1849 – around 1919), Sofia Vladimirovna (1851 – around 1919) and Nikolai Vladimirovich (1859–1917). The owners provided a warm welcome to Levitan and Kuvshinnikova, striving to create the best possible conditions for their creative work.{{Sfn|Подушков|2009}} In his memoirs, artist [[Vitold Byalynitsky-Birulya]], who visited Ostrovno in the 1900s, describes the house where Levitan lived in 1893: "The old Ushakov house in the [[Empire style]], with greenish-blue glass in the windows and a wide staircase, descending several flights and balcony platforms into the garden, was full of lilacs. It filled the greater part of the estate, and was almost the same age as the old house."{{Sfn|Турков|1974|p=80}} Levitan and Kuvshinnikova rented two rooms on the second floor. Byalynitsky-Birulya wrote: "The rooms in the house were very bright, white, without wallpaper, the windows of one room faced west, from there Levitan could watch the sunset, and the other room had a balcony and a wonderful view of the lake. In this room there was an antique [[harpsichord]]. From the balcony a staircase led to a fabulously beautiful lilac garden."{{Sfn|Подушков|2009}}

During their time at Lake Udomlya, located six kilometers from Ostrovno, Levitan and Kuvshinnikova resided at the Garusovo estate. The estate was owned by the Arakcheyevs, distant relatives of Count [[Aleksey Arakcheyev]], a prominent statesman and military figure of the early 19th century.{{Sfn|Подушков|2014|pp=116—126}} At that time, Mikhail Mikhailovich Arakcheyev (around 1850 – around 1917), who enjoyed fishing, permanently resided in the estate. The owners provided their guests with the attic on the second floor for recreational purposes. Levitan's painting ''At the Lake'' (1893), now in the [[Radishchev Art Museum]], depicts the shore of Lake Udomlya near Garusov.{{Sfn|Подушков|2009}} Kuvshinnikova said that when Levitan was working on ''Over Eternal Peace'', "the terrain and, in general, the whole motif were taken from nature during one of our rides on horseback."{{Sfn|Кац|1983|p=10}}{{Sfn|Подушков|2014|pp=116—126}} Sophia Petrovna remembered that Levitan painted the artwork "with great passion." He requested her "to play [[Ludwig van Beethoven|Beethoven]], particularly the [[Symphony No. 3 (Beethoven)|symphony héroïque]] with its [[Funeral march|Marche funèbre]]."<ref>{{Cite book |title=Исаак Ильич Левитан. Документы, материалы, библиография |date=1966 |publisher=Искусство |location=Moscow |pages=47 }}</ref>

During the creation of the canvas, Levitan produced graphic [[Sketch (drawing)|sketches]] and several [[Study (art)|studies]]. He also made a large sketch of the future painting ''Over Eternal Peace'' (canvas, oil, 95 × 197&nbsp;cm, 1893, now in the [[Tretyakov Gallery|State Tretyakov Gallery]]). In late 1893, the artist continued working on the canvas in [[Moscow]], and it was completed in early 1894.{{Sfn|Фёдоров-Давыдов|1966|p=171}} Upon finishing the work, Levitan signed it in the lower left corner: {{Nowrap|«I Levitan 94»}} (Russian «И Левитанъ 94»).<ref name=":1">{{Cite book |title=Государственная Третьяковская галерея — каталог собрания |date=2001 |publisher=Красная площадь |isbn=5-900743-56-X |volume=4: Живопись второй половины XIX века, book 1, А—М |location=Moscow |pages=360 }}</ref>

=== 22nd traveling exhibition and selling the painting ===

The canvas ''Over Eternal Peace'' was exhibited at the 22nd exhibition of the [[Society for Travelling Art Exhibitions]] ('Peredvizhniki') in Saint Petersburg on 6 March 1894<ref>{{Cite book |title=Исаак Ильич Левитан. Документы, материалы, библиография |date=1966 |publisher=Искусство |location=Moscow |pages=48–49 }}</ref> (according to other sources, 8 March),{{Sfn|Рогинская|1989|p=419}} along with other works by Levitan, including the paintings ''At the Lake'' (''At the Lake in Tver Region''), ''Evening Shadows'', ''Venice. Etude'', and several [[pastel]]s. In April of the same year, the exhibition moved to Moscow.<ref>{{Cite book |title=Товарищество передвижных художественных выставок. Письма, документы. 1869—1899 |date=1987 |publisher=Искусство |location=Moscow |pages=446}}</ref> The exhibition's Saint Petersburg segment took place in the [[Imperial Society for the Encouragement of the Arts|Society for the Encouragement of Arts]] building.{{Sfn|Рогинская|1989|p=419}} Critics highlighted several landscape paintings, including [[Ivan Shishkin]]'s ''Forest Cemetery'', [[Yefim Volkov]]'s ''Deserted Coast'', [[Nikolay Dubovskoy]]'s ''The Land'', [[Valentin Serov]]'s ''In the Crimea'', [[Nikolay Dosekin]]'s ''Surf'', and others.{{Sfn|Михеев|1894|p=123—127}}
[[File:Левитан Наозере.jpg|thumb|''At The Lake'' (canvas, oil, 109 × 163 cm, 1893, [[Radishchev Art Museum]])<ref>{{Cite web |title=Левитан Исаак Ильич — На озере (Тверская губерния), 1893 |url=http://art-catalog.ru/picture.php?id_picture=65 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180415125910/http://art-catalog.ru/picture.php?id_picture=65 |archive-date=15 April 2018 |access-date=2 October 2019 |publisher=www.art-catalog.ru}}</ref>]]
According to reports, the painting ''Over Eternal Peace'' was "significantly 
mispresented" during the exposition in Saint Petersburg.{{Sfn|Михеев|1894|p=125—126}} This was due to the organizers hanging it in a disadvantageous place for perception. Prior to the opening of the 22nd travelling exhibition in Moscow, which was to be held at the [[Moscow School of Painting, Sculpture and Architecture]], Levitan wrote to the artist [[Ilya Ostroukhov]] requesting that the painting ''Over Eternal Peace'' be displayed in an area with optimal lighting conditions for the canvas. In a letter dated March 12, 1894, Levitan wrote: "Dear Ilya Semyonovich! Tomorrow I'm leaving Moscow and will not be at the arrangement of the Moscow Itinerant, so I ask you, if you are the organizer – or even if you are not, then pass on my request to the organizers. To put my big painting either in the natural class in the place of my "At the pool," or in the place of last year's painting "Wooded shore" in the figure class."{{Sfn|Мальцева|2002|p=53}}{{Sfn|Левитан|1956|p=45}} To ensure that his enquiry reached the organizers of the exposition, Levitan sent a letter on 9 April 1894, from [[Nice]] to the artist [[Apollinary Vasnetsov]], repeating his request.{{Sfn|Левитан|1956|p=45}}

Prior to the opening of the travelling exhibition, Pavel Tretyakov purchased the painting ''Over Eternal Peace'' from the author.<ref name=":4" /> In a letter dated 18 May 1894, Levitan wrote: "I am so unspeakably happy with the knowledge that my last work will again get to you, that since yesterday I have been in some kind of ecstasy. And it is actually surprising, because you have enough of my things, but the fact that this last one has come to you touches me so much because I am all in it, with all my psyche, with all my content, and it would hurt me to tears if it had passed your colossal collection."{{Sfn|Левитан|1956|p=46—47}}
[[File:Левитан Автопортрет.jpg|left|thumb|Self-Portrait by Isaac Levitan (1890s, [[Tretyakov Gallery|State Tretyakov Gallery]])<ref>{{Cite web |title=Левитан Исаак Ильич — Автопортрет, 1890–е |url=http://art-catalog.ru/picture.php?id_picture=84 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180906043649/http://www.art-catalog.ru/picture.php?id_picture=84 |archive-date=6 September 2018 |access-date=2 October 2019 |publisher=www.art-catalog.ru}}</ref>]]
The painting ''Over Eternal Peace'' received a lot of attention from viewers and reviewers, but opinions on it were divided. Many critics characterised the painting as "causing bewilderment in the audience." Not having understood the philosophical idea that Levitan tried to put into his work, some critics found its title too pretentious and aimed at "raising the value" of the painting.{{Sfn|Фёдоров-Давыдов|1966|p=184}} For instance, the author of an article in 'Moskovskiy Listok' ({{Nowrap|No. 130}}, 11 May 1894) about the travelling exhibition wrote that "it would be better to title the painting more modestly." The author also criticized the painting for "an abyss of pretensions and lack of rights, broad ideas and poor execution." Some reviewers perceived Levitan's desire to convey his deep thoughts and experiences as an attempt to follow the fashionable "pessimistic tradition." They also felt that the title of the painting contradicted the dynamics of its depiction, which was "far from eternal peace." In particular, the author of an article in the journal "[[Nablyudatel]]" (No. 9 for 1894), wrote, calling the water space a river: "The river runs, bubbling, the clouds are painted a little too heavily on the horizon, the earth blooms, in short – life is everywhere; but the artist is possessed by pessimism, so he is in a hurry to make the viewer sad and remind him of the hour of death, if [at least] with the caption, the title of the painting"

In a [[review article]] about the Peredvizhniki exhibition published in the newspaper "[[Nedelya (newspaper)|Nedelya]]" (No. 14, 3 April 1894), the author acknowledges that the idea of the painting ''Over Eternal Peace'' is "so new and interesting that it deserves mentioning" and analysis, and concluded that "you cannot see perfection in an attempt to paint a vast space, but it shows that the artist is looking for a new path and, judging by his other, smaller works, probably will find this path."{{Sfn|Фёдоров-Давыдов|1966|p=185}} In the note "At the exhibition of the Peredvizhniki," published in the magazine "[[Vsemirnaya Illyustratsiya]]" (vol. 51, No. 1314 in 1894), the critic Vladimir Chuiko, considering the painting unsuccessful in artistic terms, at the same time recognised that "despite, however, all these technical shortcomings, the painting nevertheless has a mood: Mr Levitan has managed to express the impression of some kind of dead peace, reminiscent of the idea of death, it is only a pity that this idea is expressed so strangely."{{Sfn|Фёдоров-Давыдов|1966|p=185}}{{Sfn|Чуйко|1894|p=230}}

Several authors of publications about the travelling exhibition have noted the pictorial weakness of the painting ''Over Eternal Peace''.{{Sfn|Фёдоров-Давыдов|1966|p=185}} For example, a correspondent of the newspaper "Novosti Dnya" (No. 3903, 28 April 1894), who signed his initials M.U., noted that "the land seems as if cut out and pasted on the water, the cloud does not have its reflection in the river."{{Sfn|Фёдоров-Давыдов|1966|p=376}} Other critics believed that "the clouds are too [[ink]]y painted," that "the distant plan is too heavily painted with [[Indigo (color)|indigo]]," that the water is painted with "completely white paint," etc. According to the art historian [[Aleksei Fedorov-Davydov|Alexei Fedorov-Davydov]], "the new character of the painting with its decorative features and the desire to solve monumental tasks in this way was taken for a strange oddity, for incompleteness, for "indefinite ointment," posing as a special "manner," for the laying of new paths."{{Sfn|Фёдоров-Давыдов|1966|p=185}}

In periodicals of that time, there were also openly unfriendly publications. The authors of these publications attempted to blatantly insult the artist and his work. Thus, in "Peterbugskiy Listok" (No. 67, 10 March 1894) under the signature "Realist" was published a [[quatrain]], addressed to Levitan, of the following content: "You smeared a lot ... You have not glorified yourself. / Pastels, canvases – worthy of a smile. / "Over Eternal Peace" accidentally you / Put rocks in the sky, my friend, by mistake." Another example of this is a [[feuilleton]] by journalist [[Vlas Doroshevich]] published in the "[[Peterburgskaya Gazeta]]" (No. 77, 20 March 1894), "Mobile Geniuses. Misunderstanding in Act I," which took the form of a dialogue between [[Isaac Levitan]] and the critic [[Vladimir Stasov]]. As a result of their "discussion" Levitan's painting "is recognised as suitable only for making Stasov's [[sirwal]] out of its canvas."{{Sfn|Фёдоров-Давыдов|1966|p=376}}

Nevertheless, there were also positive reviews of the painting ''Over Eternal Peace''. In particular, in a review article about the 22nd travelling exhibition, published in the newspaper "[[Russkie Vedomosti]]" (No. 110, 23 April 1894), art critic Vladimir Sizov called Levitan's painting "well thought out and strongly felt," distinguished by "undoubted artistic merits."{{Sfn|Сизов|1894|p=3—4}}{{Sfn|Фёдоров-Давыдов|1966|p=185}} In an article published in 1894 in the magazine "[[The Artist (Russian magazine)|The Artist]]," writer and publicist Vasily Mikheev "sensitively grasped the deep psychology of the canvas and called it a genuine landscape-painting, remarkable for its content and 'mood'."{{Sfn|Фёдоров-Давыдов|1966|p=185}}

=== Subsequent events ===
''Over Eternal Peace'' was exhibited at an 1898 exhibition of Russian and [[Grand Duchy of Finland|Finnish]] artists as part of the [[Munich Secession]]. The exhibition, which showcased around 120 paintings, was held in [[Munich]] from 1 May to 1 June and later visited [[Berlin]], [[Cologne]] and [[Düsseldorf]]. Levitan's paintings ''The Last Snow'', ''Meadow on the Edge of the Forest'' and ''In the North'' were also featured in the exhibition.{{Sfn|Харитонова|2018|p=20}}
[[File:Above eternal rest by Isaac Levitan in the State Tretyakov Gallery IMG 7993 ed.jpg|thumb|The painting ''Over Eternal Peace'' in the Levitan Hall of the [[Tretyakov Gallery|State Tretyakov Gallery]]]]
During the summer of 1941, following the start of the [[Great Patriotic War (term)|Great Patriotic War]], numerous exhibits from the State Tretyakov Gallery's collection were evacuated to [[Novosibirsk]]. They were stored in the [[unfinished building]] of the [[Novosibirsk Opera and Ballet Theatre]]. One of the evacuated works of art was Levitan's painting ''Over Eternal Peace''.<ref>{{Cite book |url=https://vivaldi.nlr.ru/bv000070113/view/?#page=1021 |title=Энциклопедия Новосибирска |date=2003 |publisher=Новосибирское книжное издательство |isbn=5-7620-0968-8 |location=Novosibirsk |pages=989 }}</ref> The paintings were eventually returned to the Tretyakov Gallery in November 1944.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Чернова |first=К. |date=6 May 2015 |title=Пункт назначения — Новосибирск |url=http://www.sovsibir.ru/news/151318 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190324203038/http://www.sovsibir.ru/news/151318 |archive-date=24 March 2019 |access-date=22 September 2019 |publisher=Советская Сибирь — www.sovsibir.ru}}</ref>

The painting ''Over Eternal Peace'' was exhibited at several exhibitions, including Levitan's personal exhibitions in 1938 at the State Tretyakov Gallery in Moscow and in 1939 at the [[Russian Museum|State Russian Museum]] in [[Leningrad]]. It was also displayed at the jubilee exhibition commemorating the 100th anniversary of the artist's birth, held in 1960–1961 in Moscow, Leningrad, and [[Kyiv]].<ref name=":1" /><ref>{{Cite book |title=Государственная Третьяковская галерея — каталог собрания |date=2001 |publisher=Красная площадь |isbn=5-900743-56-X |volume=4: Живопись второй половины XIX века, book 1, А—М |location=Moscow |pages=463 }}</ref> The canvas was exhibited in 1971–1972 at "Peredvizhniki in the State Tretyakov Gallery" (Moscow) and "Landscape Painting of the Peredvizhniki" (Kyiv, Leningrad, [[Minsk]], Moscow) to commemorate the centenary of the [[Peredvizhniki|Society for Travelling Art Exhibitions]].<ref name=":1" /><ref>{{Cite book |title=Государственная Третьяковская галерея — каталог собрания |date=2001 |publisher=Красная площадь |isbn=5-900743-56-X |volume=4: Живопись второй половины XIX века, book 1, А—М |location=Moscow |pages=471 }}</ref> The painting was exhibited at the [[New Tretyakov Gallery]] on [[Krymsky Val]] for the 150th anniversary exhibition of Levitan's birth from October 2010 to March 2011.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Махонин |first=А. |date=18 October 2010 |title=В Третьяковской галерее юбилейная выставка Исаака Левитана |url=https://www.vedomosti.ru/lifestyle/articles/2010/10/18/unylaya_pora_ochej |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20151003035548/http://www.vedomosti.ru/lifestyle/articles/2010/10/18/unylaya_pora_ochej |archive-date=3 October 2015 |access-date=1 October 2019 |publisher=Ведомости — www.vedomosti.ru}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Исаак Левитан. К 150-летию со дня рождения |url=http://www.museum.ru/N41169 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170504041321/http://www.museum.ru/N41169 |archive-date=4 May 2017 |access-date=1 October 2019 |publisher=Музеи России — www.museum.ru}}</ref> It was also displayed at the [[Vatican Museums]] from November 2018 to February 2019 as part of the exhibition "Pilgrimage of Russian Art. From [[Dionisius]] to Malevich."<ref>{{Cite web |last=Уваров |first=С.А. |date=20 November 2018 |title=От нашего Рима к вашему: Третьяковка привезла свои шедевры в Ватикан |url=https://iz.ru/814194/sergei-uvarov/doroga-k-khramu-tretiakovka-privezla-svoi-shedevry-v-vatikan |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190814221918/https://iz.ru/814194/sergei-uvarov/doroga-k-khramu-tretiakovka-privezla-svoi-shedevry-v-vatikan |archive-date=14 August 2019 |access-date=1 October 2019 |publisher=Известия — iz.ru}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Выставка "Русский путь. От Дионисия до Малевича" в Музеях Ватикана |url=http://www.museum.ru/N71374 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181115122017/http://museum.ru/N71374 |archive-date=15 November 2018 |access-date=1 October 2019 |publisher=Музеи России — www.museum.ru}}</ref>

The painting ''Over Eternal Peace'' is currently on display in the Levitan Hall (Room No. 37) of the main building of the [[Tretyakov Gallery]] in [[Lavrushinsky Lane]].<ref name=":0" /><ref>{{Cite web |title=Левитан И. И. "Над вечным покоем" |url=https://www.msk-guide.ru/page_21410.htm |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191001035413/https://www.msk-guide.ru/page_21410.htm |archive-date=1 October 2019 |access-date=30 September 2019 |publisher=www.msk-guide.ru}}</ref>

== Description ==
The painting portrays "a gigantic turning of the lake's water space,"{{Sfn|Фёдоров-Давыдов|1966|p=172}} the vast expanse of which resembles a wide-spreading river.{{Sfn|Мальцева|2002|p=29}} Above the lake, there is a "majestic expanse of sky with swirling clouds colliding with each other."{{Sfn|Фёдоров-Давыдов|1966|p=172}} The image of the sky can be divided into two parts: darker, cloudy at the bottom and lighter, cloudy at the top.{{Sfn|Фёдоров-Давыдов|1966|p=181}} In the near background, there is a cape with an old church on its elevated shore. To the left of the church lies a rural cemetery with old, long-forgotten graves.{{Sfn|Фёдоров-Давыдов|1966|p=172}}{{Sfn|Мальцева|2002|p=29}} The cemetery creates a sombre atmosphere with ruined graves almost flattened to the ground, gnarled crosses, and overgrown paths. The artist has paid particular attention to the composition of the cemetery church on the hill. The old wooden church standing by the cliff is "perfectly integrated into the landscape," and the trees growing near it are painted with a soft brush.{{Sfn|Мальцева|2002|p=29}} According to the art historian Alexei Fedorov-Davydov, "a small cape with a church and a cemetery, booed by the wind of the elements, seems like the prow of some kind of ship moving into the unknown distance," and the light in the church window is perceived as "a witness to human life, its inexhaustibility and quiet resignation."{{Sfn|Фёдоров-Давыдов|1966|p=174}} Fedorov-Davydov noted that in the painting we see "a clear construction of the image from the main parts and different detailing of each of them"; all this corresponds to the compositional solution of the canvas, in which "asymmetry is balanced by the antidirectional movement of each of these parts that make up the painting – the cape, the islet, the water, the clouds, etc."{{Sfn|Фёдоров-Давыдов|1966|p=182—184}}
[[File:Levitan nad vech pok28 (cropped).jpg|left|thumb|Church and cemetery (fragment of the painting)]]
The composition found by the artist required a pictorial solution that would help "to feel more acutely the vastness and power of the space that opens up before the observer." This seems to explain the differences in the way Levitan painted the near and far plans: the latter are "given with such an extreme generalisation of form and colour that we have not seen in his previous works."{{Sfn|Мальцева|2002|p=29}} In addition, the painting techniques used are diverse. The dark and stormy sky with low-hanging clouds is emphasised by a layer of paint applied over the [[Horizon|horizon line]]. The brushstrokes become more dynamic for the higher parts of the sky, and the texture of the painting changes. The movement of the disorderly pile of clouds is conveyed by "a combination of subtle shades of greyish purple, in some places leaden or greyish-brown tones." The painting's lower planes exhibit a range of painting techniques. Levitan used different methods to paint the water, the low bank in the distance, and the grassy hill in the foreground.{{Sfn|Мальцева|2002|p=29}} To prevent the water from appearing monochromatic, he employed grey and white tones to depict the ripples, varied the direction of the strokes, and then scratched (apparently with a comb) sections of the water surface over an undried layer of paint.{{Sfn|Фёдоров-Давыдов|1966|p=182}}{{Sfn|Карлсен|Кочик|Виктурина|1963|p=34}} According to art historian Faina Maltseva, "the difference in painting techniques does not, however, violate the ideological [[pathos]] of the idea or the persuasiveness of its interpretation that includes real nature impressions taken as a basis for the plot of the painting."{{Sfn|Мальцева|2002|p=29}}

There are different versions as to which place is depicted on the canvas and which church served as the prototype for the one painted by the artist. In her often quoted memoirs that "have become a [[chrestomathy]]," Sophia Kuvshinnikova claims that the painting ''Over Eternal Peace'' was painted near Lake Udomlya, "only the church in nature was different, ugly, and Levitan replaced it with a cosy church from [[Plyos, Ivanovo Oblast|Plyos]]."{{Sfn|Левитан|1956|p=171}}{{Sfn|Кац|1983|p=10}} According to experts on Levitan's work, it is believed that he used his 1888 sketch "Wooden Church in Plyos at the last rays of the sun" as a reference when painting the church.{{Sfn|Фёдоров-Давыдов|1966|p=176}}{{Sfn|Мальцева|2002|p=29}} The sketch depicted the Peter and Paul Church, which was situated on a hill that would later be named "Mount Levitan." In 1903, the Peter and Paul Church was destroyed by fire. In 1982, the Resurrection Church from the village of Bilyukovo was relocated to the same spot. It was informally named "Over Eternal Peace."<ref>{{Cite book |last=Чурюканова |first=О.В. |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=wjVfDwAAQBAJ&pg=PT30 |title=Храмы Плёса. На Золотом кольце России |date=2018 |publisher=Litres |isbn=9785041183974 |location=Moscow |pages=30–31}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Плёс. Церковь Воскресения Христова из с. Билюково |url=http://plesru.ru/Arhitect/voskresenia.htm |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190328001409/http://plesru.ru/Arhitect/voskresenia.htm |archive-date=28 March 2019 |access-date=28 September 2019 |publisher=plesru.ru |language=ru}}</ref>
[[File:Levitan Wooden church in Plyos at the sunset.jpg|thumb|''Wooden church in Plyos in the last rays of the sun'' (sketch, 1888, private collection)]]
The memoirs of the artist [[Vitold Byalynitsky-Birulya]] give a different version. According to him, Levitan often came with his paints and [[easel]] to the shore of Lake Ostrovno, "near which, on a hill, stood an old wooden church, half buried in the ground." Byalynitsky-Birulya gave the following details: "Near the church, closer to the lake, there was a forgotten, completely overgrown cemetery. The wooden crosses were shrivelled and covered with green moss."<ref name=":2">{{Cite book |title=Мастера советского искусства о пейзаже |date=1963 |publisher=Издательство Академии художеств СССР |location=Moscow |pages=37 }}</ref>{{Sfn|Кац|1983|p=10}}{{Sfn|Подушков|2014|pp=116—126}} The artist also noted that "Levitan liked to visit the neighbouring Lake Udomlya, he liked to go by boat to the oval island, from where the whole length of Lake Udomlya was visible." According to Byalynitsky-Birulya, "the cape of the island was taken as the first plan of the painting, but it was supplemented by the motif of a church and a cemetery observed on Lake Ostrovno."<ref name=":2" />{{Sfn|Кац|1983|p=10}} According to one account, the "oval island," the cape of which is depicted in the painting could be Arzhanik Island, located in the northern part of Lake Udomlya, not far from Garusov.{{Sfn|Иванов|1963|p=174}}{{Sfn|Подушков|2009}} According to other information, it could be Dvinovo Island, located in the southern part of Udomlya Lake. Nowadays this island is covered with forest, but at the end of the 19th century it was "completely treeless."<ref>{{Cite book |last=Збарский |first=И.Б. |url=https://archive.org/details/obektn10000zbar/page/250 |title=Объект № 1 |date=2000 |publisher=Вагриус |isbn=9785264001772 |series=Мой 20 век |location=Moscow |pages=250 |language=ru}}</ref>

In the 1983 book "Artists of the Udomlya Region," the art historian Leah Katz published an excerpt from the notes of Alexei Moravov (son of the artist [[Alexander Moravov]]), which gives another version. According to him, at the time when Levitan was working in the Udomlya Lake area, the only wooden church on the lake shore was "between the villages of Akulovo and Troitsa, towards Lubenkino," and in the early 1900s it was "dismantled and moved to the other side of the lake, to the village of Ryad," where it burned down some time later. At the same time, Moravov confirmed that there was no church on the island of Udomlya.{{Sfn|Кац|1983|p=10}}

Local historian Dmitry Podushkov noted that "we can say with a high degree of certainty that the composition of the painting ''Over Eternal Peace'' was made by Levitan in the village of Ostrovno," where the Ushakov estate served as an upper vantage point. At the same time, according to Podushkov, "the vast expanse of water, the general contours of the lake shore, the viewer's vantage point again, the distant horizon line, some elements of the composition can be seen on Lake Udomlya." In this case, according to Podushkov, the high point of observation could be Krasilnikova Hill, a high hill at the northern end of Lake Udomlya, which offers a panoramic view of the lake in a south-easterly direction.{{Sfn|Подушков|2009}}

Art historian Alexei Fedorov-Davydov wrote that the painting ''Over Eternal Peace'' is "a transfer of a motif seen on one lake to the image of another, similar one."{{Sfn|Фёдоров-Давыдов|1966|p=175}} At the same time, in his opinion, the natural view of Lake Udomlya is "the frame of the work, its compositional basis."{{Sfn|Фёдоров-Давыдов|1966|p=178}} Fedorov-Davydov agreed with Byalynitsky-Birulya that the painting "was not given to Levitan thanks to this or that sketch," "it was not created from a study and, of course, it is not an enlarged study." According to Fedorov-Davydov, "in the end the painting seems to be a much more composed landscape than it really is,"{{Sfn|Фёдоров-Давыдов|1966|p=175}} and the degree of "composition" in Levitan's paintings is measured by "the degree of synthesis of the real view into a pictorial landscape image."{{Sfn|Фёдоров-Давыдов|1966|p=180}}

== Sketches and Studies ==
[[File:Levitan Before a thunderstorm study12.jpg|thumb|''Before the Thunderstorm'' (initial sketches-variants of the painting ''Over Eternal Peace'', 1893, [[Tretyakov Gallery|State Tretyakov Gallery]])]]
The [[Tretyakov Gallery|State Tretyakov Gallery]] holds two original graphic sketches-variants for the painting ''Over Eternal Peace''. Dating from 1893 and bearing the common title ''Before the Storm'', the drawings are executed in graphite pencil on a single sheet of paper (total format 18 × 16&nbsp;cm, inv. no. 5689).<ref>{{Cite web |title=Левитан Исаак Ильич — Перед грозой, 1893 (первоначальный эскиз-вариант) |url=http://art-catalog.ru/picture.php?id_picture=250 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150419071946/http://www.art-catalog.ru/picture.php?id_picture=250 |archive-date=19 April 2015 |access-date=24 August 2019 |publisher=www.art-catalog.ru}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Левитан Исаак Ильич — Перед грозой, 1893 (эскиз-вариант) |url=http://art-catalog.ru/picture.php?id_picture=219 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150421183439/http://art-catalog.ru/picture.php?id_picture=219 |archive-date=21 April 2015 |access-date=24 August 2019 |publisher=www.art-catalog.ru}}</ref> The upper drawing is considered to be the first and the lower the second. According to the art historian Faina Maltseva, these "hasty sketches" are "not even sketches yet, but only the first born thought, which contains the grain of the future painting." Levitan shows the anxiety in nature associated with the approaching storm. The lower sketch depicts the lake from a higher viewpoint than the upper one, creating a grander impression of the water spaces. The artist's haste is evident in the uneven and jagged strokes, as well as in the outline of the depicted objects.{{Sfn|Мальцева|2002|p=28}} According to Elena Byalynitsky-Birulya (widow of the artist [[Vitold Byalynitsky-Birulya]]), these drawings portray the landscape of Lake Ostrovno with the Trinity Church on the shore.{{Sfn|Фёдоров-Давыдов|1966|p=175}}

In 1893, while working on the painting ''Over Eternal Peace'', Levitan created several sketches. One of these sketches, titled ''Cloudy Sky'' (paper on cardboard, oil, 17.8 × 26.2&nbsp;cm),<ref name=":5">{{Cite book |title=Исаак Ильич Левитан. Документы, материалы, библиография |date=1966 |publisher=Искусство |location=Moscow |pages=107 }}</ref> is held in the collection of the [[Russian Museum|State Russian Museum]]{{Sfn|Фёдоров-Давыдов|1966|p=170}}{{Sfn|Фёдоров-Давыдов|1966|p=398}}{{Sfn|Мальцева|2002|p=29}}<ref>{{Cite web |title=Левитан Исаак Ильич — Облачное небо. Сумерки, 1893 (этюд) |url=http://art-catalog.ru/picture.php?id_picture=300 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160712001824/http://art-catalog.ru/picture.php?id_picture=300 |archive-date=12 July 2016 |access-date=24 August 2019 |publisher=www.art-catalog.ru}}</ref> (inv. Zh-1218), having entered it in 1927. Prior to this, it was part of K.D. Ermakov's collection.<ref name=":5" /> Another sketch, titled ''Sky'', is held in a private collection in Moscow{{Sfn|Мальцева|2002|p=29}} (as of 1966, it was part of R.K. Viktorova's collection).{{Sfn|Фёдоров-Давыдов|1966|p=176}}{{Sfn|Фёдоров-Давыдов|1966|p=398}} These sketches depict a stormy sky and were created by the artist at different times. One of them, titled ''Cloudy Sky'', shows dark purple clouds in a blue sky, while the other, titled ''Sky'', shows grey clouds with yellow and pink colours used to paint the sky.{{Sfn|Фёдоров-Давыдов|1966|p=176}} Another sketch, titled ''Lake'', is also held in a private collection in Moscow{{Sfn|Мальцева|2002|p=29}} (as of 1966, in the collection of L.F. Ilyichev).{{Sfn|Фёдоров-Давыдов|1966|p=176}}{{Sfn|Фёдоров-Давыдов|1966|p=398}}
[[File:Isaac Levitan - Au-dessus du repos éternel.jpg|left|thumb|''Over Eternal Peace'' (large sketch, 1893, [[Tretyakov Gallery|State Tretyakov Gallery]])]]
During the summer of 1893, Levitan produced a pastel titled ''Forgotten'' (current location unknown). The artwork portrays "an abandoned cemetery on a slope with drooping crosses."{{Sfn|Фёдоров-Давыдов|1986|pp=208—209}} Alexei Fedorov-Davydov stated that "the motif was definitely used in the painting ''Over Eternal Peace'', including the grave crosses looming against the water."{{Sfn|Фёдоров-Давыдов|1986|p=209}} The pastel ''Forgotten'' was exhibited at the 13th periodic exhibition of the [[Moscow Society of Art Lovers]] in 1893–1894 and at Levitan's posthumous exhibition in 1901.<ref>{{Cite book |title=Исаак Ильич Левитан. Документы, материалы, библиография |date=1966 |publisher=Искусство |location=Moscow |pages=106 }}</ref>

In 1893, Levitan created a large pictorial sketch for the future painting ''Over Eternal Peace'' (canvas, oil, 95 × 197&nbsp;cm, State Tretyakov Gallery, inv. 1487).<ref name=":1" /><ref>{{Cite web |title=Левитан Исаак Ильич — Над вечным покоем, 1893—1894 (эскиз) |url=http://art-catalog.ru/picture.php?id_picture=67 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150421175944/http://art-catalog.ru/picture.php?id_picture=67 |archive-date=21 April 2015 |access-date=24 August 2019 |publisher=www.art-catalog.ru}}</ref> The composition was based on the second variant sketch ''Before the Thunderstorm'', but with some alterations. The proportion of water surface in the right part was reduced, the distant islet was transformed into a [[Spit (landform)|spit]] connected to the shore, and "the church in the [[Empire style]] was replaced by an old wooden [[kletsky church]]."{{Sfn|Фёдоров-Давыдов|1966|pp=176—177}} In comparison to the final version of the painting, the large sketch – particularly in the right-hand section – has a significantly larger area dedicated to the foreground shore.{{Sfn|Фёдоров-Давыдов|1986|p=209}} This sketch was displayed at an exhibition of Russian and Finnish artists in Saint Petersburg in 1898.<ref name=":1" /><ref name=":5" /> Sharing his impressions of the exhibition, the artist [[Nikolay Dubovskoy]] wrote in a letter to [[Nikolay Kasatkin]] on 20 January 1898 that it "impresses with the talent of the exhibitors" and that "the best thing at the exhibition is Levitan's ''Over Eternal Peace'' (sketch of a painting)."{{Sfn|Левитан|1956|p=275}} The sketch was purchased by Pavel Tretyakov at the exhibition and, after the collector's death in 1899, his heirs transferred it to the Tretyakov Gallery.<ref name=":1" />

Furthermore, researchers of Levitan's work consider the sketch ''Wooden Church in Plyos at the Last Rays of the Sun'', painted in 1888 (paper on cardboard, oil, 14 × 24&nbsp;cm, private collection), a preparatory material for the painting ''Over Eternal Peace''.{{Sfn|Фёдоров-Давыдов|1966|p=176}}{{Sfn|Мальцева|2002|p=29}}{{Sfn|Левитан|1956|p=185}}<ref>{{Cite web |title=Левитан Исаак Ильич — Деревянная церковь в Плёсе при последних лучах солнца, 1888 (этюд) |url=http://art-catalog.ru/picture.php?id_picture=7392 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150419071127/http://www.art-catalog.ru/picture.php?id_picture=7392 |archive-date=19 April 2015 |access-date=31 August 2019 |publisher=www.art-catalog.ru}}</ref> This sketch was acquired by collector [[Aleksey Petrovich Langovoy|Aleksey Langovoy]] from the author in 1898. Langovoy recalled that the work had previously been in the Tretyakov Gallery. On the advice of the artist [[Mikhail Nesterov]], Levitan used it to paint the church in his painting ''Over Eternal Peace''. According to Langovoy, "Levitan followed this advice, took back his sketch from P.M. Tretyakov and painted, instead of the usual rural church, this old church of Plyos," whereupon Tretyakov "told Levitan that this sketch had become unnecessary for him and offered to take it back and replace it with another of his choice."{{Sfn|Левитан|1956|p=185}}
